    "updated_at": "2016-05-18T12:01:58Z",
    "body": "See https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/comms.html#feedback-from-the-protocol\n\nIn particular it says \"queue a task\", which means that the `open` and `message` events will not be fired until the event loop spins, which allows the script that creates the `WebSocket` to finish and set up all the relevant listeners.\n\nYou should even be able to set up the `message` listener inside the `open` listener, per spec, without losing any `message` events. But if you set it up in a timeout, there are no guarantees, same with all other events in the Web platform.\n\nSo you should be able to send messages from the server directly, along with the server's open handshake.\n\n> As a sidenote, it appears that in the MessagePort spec, the connection must be opened explicitly using .start() or implicitly by setting the .onmessage handler.\n\nAlthough WebSocket uses `MessageEvent` for its `message` event, it does not use port message queues, so there's no `.start()` for WebSockets. Using `addEventListener()` works just as well for WebSocket.\n\nDoes this make it clear enough? Would you like to have some notes in the spec about this?\n",
